<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hird</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as hirð, De Håndgangne Menn</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The hird was, in Scandinavian history, at first an informal retinue of armed companions, the hirdmen or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/housecarl">housecarls</a>, and later the core of the royal army and a formal royal court household. The word comes from Old Norse hirð, from Old English hir(e)d, 'household, retinue', or perhaps a German cognate. By the reign of Håkon IV (1204–1263) the Norwegian hird had subdivisions on continental patterns, squires, men-at-arms and knights, its law code, the Hirdskraa, was read when the whole hird assembled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> and Easter, and its upper ranks supplied royal officials. Under Håkon V (1299–1319) its Norse titles gave way to continental ones, and the king was technically first among equals.</p></div>
